 ## Background
-The Watt & Watt Marching Cubes (WWMC) algorithm was introduced by D. D'Agostino, A. Clematis, and V. Gianuzzi in 2000 [S1]. It is named after Watt & Watt because the first three issues addressed (topological consistency, performance, and vertex deduplication) were included in the version proposed in [27] (Watt & Watt, 1992), while the fourth issue (I/O buffering) was added by the authors [S1]. The algorithm addresses issues widely discussed in literature regarding the original Lorensen and Cline proposal [S1]. It is used in 3D data analysis workflows, particularly in medical image processing and scientific visualization, where efficient isosurface extraction from large volumetric datasets is required [S1].
+The Watt & Watt Marching Cubes (WWMC) algorithm was introduced by D. D’Agostino, A. Clematis, and V. Gianuzzi in their 2006 paper "Parallel Isosurface Extraction for 3D Data Analysis Workflows in Distributed Environments" [S1]. The algorithm addresses issues identified in the original Lorensen and Cline Marching Cubes proposal, incorporating solutions from prior literature: the modified lookup table from [25] for topological consistency, and the vertex deduplication technique from [27] for performance [S1]. It is used in medical image processing, fluid dynamics simulations, and bioinformatics applications where efficient isosurface extraction from large volumetric datasets is required [S1].
 
